```javascript
self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(
    caches.open('v1').then(async cache => {
      const cached = await cache.match(event.request);
      const networkFetch = fetch(event.request).then(response => {
        cache.put(event.request, response.clone()); // clone: body can only be read once
        return response;
      });
      // Serve cached immediately if present; otherwise wait on the network
      return cached || networkFetch;
    })
  );
});
```

Two details that matter in real implementations: `response.clone()` is required because a `Response` body is a stream that can only be consumed once — you need one copy to return to the page and one to store in the cache. And the network fetch still runs even when serving from cache, specifically so the **next** request gets fresher data — that's the "revalidate" half of the strategy's name.
